431 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H4/T(Anthropogenic drivers such as factory farming, habitat loss, and biodiversity loss will further exacerbate the risk of zoonotic spillover and pandemics. The estimated annual losses associated with future pandemics are immense\204around 0.6% of global income per year\204further strengthening the argument for a comprehensive risk assessment tool.3 )/E(Anthropogenic drivers such as factory farming, habitat loss, and biodiversity loss will further exacerbate the risk of zoonotic spillover and pandemics. The estimated annual losses associated with future pandemics are immense\204around 0.6% of global income per year\204further strengthening the argument for a comprehensive risk assessment tool.3 )/Pg 13 0 R/K[3 4]>>
endobj

432 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H4/T(Little action has been taken to better understand pandemic risk or to prepare for future pandemics. Fragmented, incomplete research and assessment and modeling tools that are limited in scope have created a pressing need to better understand what drives pandemic risk. This long under-prioritized area of research and policy requires a unified scientific mechanism to assess and communicate pandemic risk. Rather than perpetuating cycles of attention and neglect, there is a growing consensus that sustained scientific research, global coordination, and forward-looking mechanisms to translate research into policy are necessary to prevent avoidable pandemic threats. )/E(Little action has been taken to better understand pandemic risk or to prepare for future pandemics. Fragmented, incomplete research and assessment and modeling tools that are limited in scope have created a pressing need to better understand what drives pandemic risk. This long under-prioritized area of research and policy requires a unified scientific mechanism to assess and communicate pandemic risk. Rather than perpetuating cycles of attention and neglect, there is a growing consensus that sustained scientific research, global coordination, and forward-looking mechanisms to translate research into policy are necessary to prevent avoidable pandemic threats. )/Pg 13 0 R/K[5 6]>>
endobj

433 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/T(The purpose of a pandemic risk assessment \(PRA\) framework is to move beyond reactive preparedness for health emergencies and toward identifying, prioritizing, and addressing pandemic threats before they escalate. )/E(The purpose of a pandemic risk assessment \(PRA\) framework is to move beyond reactive preparedness for health emergencies and toward identifying, prioritizing, and addressing pandemic threats before they escalate. )/Pg 13 0 R/K[1 2]>>
endobj

434 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/Figure/P 279 0 R/Pg 13 0 R/K[14]>>
endobj

435 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/T(The existing PRA and preparedness landscape is made up of a diverse set of frameworks, including WHO-led accountability and monitoring instruments, pathogen-focused influenza risk assessments, mobility and modeling-based platforms, global comparative readiness indices, and strategic monitoring efforts. However, each of these tools only captures a fragment of the overall issue. There is currently no assessment that can comprehensively take stock of all the drivers of pandemic risk, and reliably predict the likelihood of the next pandemic or its probable characteristics. )/E(The existing PRA and preparedness landscape is made up of a diverse set of frameworks, including WHO-led accountability and monitoring instruments, pathogen-focused influenza risk assessments, mobility and modeling-based platforms, global comparative readiness indices, and strategic monitoring efforts. However, each of these tools only captures a fragment of the overall issue. There is currently no assessment that can comprehensively take stock of all the drivers of pandemic risk, and reliably predict the likelihood of the next pandemic or its probable characteristics. )/Pg 14 0 R/K[2 3 4]>>
endobj

436 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/T(Additionally, tools that evaluate preparedness typically only capture country-level capacity without subnational detail, depend on unevenly available public data, potentially incorporate expert bias or unreliable self-evaluative bias, and do not link capacity to dynamic transmission or operational decision-making. Tools that aim to characterize pathogen risk remain limited because many are qualitative in nature and focus only on specific pathogens or regions.)/E(Additionally, tools that evaluate preparedness typically only capture country-level capacity without subnational detail, depend on unevenly available public data, potentially incorporate expert bias or unreliable self-evaluative bias, and do not link capacity to dynamic transmission or operational decision-making. Tools that aim to characterize pathogen risk remain limited because many are qualitative in nature and focus only on specific pathogens or regions.)/Pg 14 0 R>>
endobj

437 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/T(While the landscape is populated with multiple frameworks, none provide globally integrated, validated, actionable, and predictive insight across pathogen emergence, transmission potential, and real-world policy levers. As such, our review seeks to map this landscape across major frameworks, identify primary limitations, and deliver design principles that a future PRA should address to close these gaps. )/E(While the landscape is populated with multiple frameworks, none provide globally integrated, validated, actionable, and predictive insight across pathogen emergence, transmission potential, and real-world policy levers. As such, our review seeks to map this landscape across major frameworks, identify primary limitations, and deliver design principles that a future PRA should address to close these gaps. )/Pg 14 0 R/K[6 7]>>
endobj

438 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/T(Our Ask)/E(Our Ask)/Pg 14 0 R/K[8]>>
endobj

439 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/T(The United Nations \(UN\) Foundation approached our team looking to understand what tools already existed for assessing pandemic risk, and where their biggest pitfalls were for effecting policy change. A scoping review on the existing tools for assessing pandemic risk has never been performed before. Using the findings from this review, the UN Foundation will, in partnership with the National Academy of Medicine, aim to put forth a framework for a scientific agenda for Pandemic Risk Assessment that is accessible to policymakers. Furthermore, the UN Foundation hopes to leverage these findings to integrate risk assessment into discussions at the 2026 High Level Meeting on Pandemic Preparedness and Response at the United Nations General Assembly.)/E(The United Nations \(UN\) Foundation approached our team looking to understand what tools already existed for assessing pandemic risk, and where their biggest pitfalls were for effecting policy change. A scoping review on the existing tools for assessing pandemic risk has never been performed before. Using the findings from this review, the UN Foundation will, in partnership with the National Academy of Medicine, aim to put forth a framework for a scientific agenda for Pandemic Risk Assessment that is accessible to policymakers. Furthermore, the UN Foundation hopes to leverage these findings to integrate risk assessment into discussions at the 2026 High Level Meeting on Pandemic Preparedness and Response at the United Nations General Assembly.)/Pg 14 0 R/K[5]>>
endobj

