<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/T(The 27 completed interviews provided diverse perspectives on the limitations of current preparedness assessment tools, the technical and political challenges of measuring pandemic risk, and the design features that would ensure a future PRA tool is actionable for policymakers and other stakeholders. Insights from these conversations informed both our evaluation of existing tools and our design recommendations for future PRA approaches. )/E(The 27 completed interviews provided diverse perspectives on the limitations of current preparedness assessment tools, the technical and political challenges of measuring pandemic risk, and the design features that would ensure a future PRA tool is actionable for policymakers and other stakeholders. Insights from these conversations informed both our evaluation of existing tools and our design recommendations for future PRA approaches. )/Pg 18 0 R/K[32 33]>>
endobj

509 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/T(In conducting our consultations, we followed a semi-structured approach that provided our interviewers with guiding questions, while also allowing each interview to be tailored to the background of the expert. Each conversation was anchored in a core set of )/E(In conducting our consultations, we followed a semi-structured approach that provided our interviewers with guiding questions, while also allowing each interview to be tailored to the background of the expert. Each conversation was anchored in a core set of )/Pg 18 0 R/K[34]>>
endobj

510 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/Figure/P 284 0 R/Pg 18 0 R/K[37]>>
endobj

511 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/T(questions that centered around strengths and limitations of existing preparedness assessment tools, the types of risk outputs and indicators that would be most useful for decision-makers, and the areas that a future PRA should prioritize, such as spillover, emergence, spread, or financing gaps. In many consultations, we also invited interviewees to reflect on the institutional arrangements that could lend a PRA tool more credibility and ensure political uptake, as well as how to support communication of findings to various audiences. This adaptive approach allowed us to gather comparable insights across interviews while still capturing the nuanced perspectives of experts with varied roles in the global health sphere.)/E(questions that centered around strengths and limitations of existing preparedness assessment tools, the types of risk outputs and indicators that would be most useful for decision-makers, and the areas that a future PRA should prioritize, such as spillover, emergence, spread, or financing gaps. In many consultations, we also invited interviewees to reflect on the institutional arrangements that could lend a PRA tool more credibility and ensure political uptake, as well as how to support communication of findings to various audiences. This adaptive approach allowed us to gather comparable insights across interviews while still capturing the nuanced perspectives of experts with varied roles in the global health sphere.)/Pg 19 0 R/K[1]>>
endobj

