<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/T(One of the first-developed tools in our dataset was the Moody\220s RMS Infectious Disease Model \(RMS-IDM\), developed to help corporate risk managers and insurers quantify risk and anticipate potential financial losses that could arise for their clients as a result of shocks from epidemics and pandemics. First created in 2006, Moody\220s Corporation has updated the tool to incorporate new and emerging variables into their models, such as improvements to global vaccine manufacturing and subnational variation in disease vulnerability. The model has also developed to differentiate between \215coronaviruses,\216 \215other respiratory diseases,\216 and \215non-respiratory emerging pathogens,\216 allowing for a breadth of potential pandemic threats while maintaining granularity in the types of pathogens assessed. The utility of this framework, especially as it continues to incorporate more robust and topically-relevant variables, is that it is based in epidemiological modeling and captures variation at the subnational level, thus providing a complex picture of how an emerging pandemic threat may endanger countries. Another key benefit of the model is that its results are formatted for a non-epidemiological audience. While its key end-users are those in risk management, it may be well-adapted for engagements with political stakeholders aiming to quantify the economic and financial risk of an early pandemic threat. This tool also uniquely employs probabilistic modeling to project risk. However, due to the proprietary nature of the model, information on the predictive capacity of this tool is limited. This tool may only work to retroactively assess previous pandemics and characterize the probable extent of pandemics when in their early stages.10, 11, 46)/E(One of the first-developed tools in our dataset was the Moody\220s RMS Infectious Disease Model \(RMS-IDM\), developed to help corporate risk managers and insurers quantify risk and anticipate potential financial losses that could arise for their clients as a result of shocks from epidemics and pandemics. First created in 2006, Moody\220s Corporation has updated the tool to incorporate new and emerging variables into their models, such as improvements to global vaccine manufacturing and subnational variation in disease vulnerability. The model has also developed to differentiate between \215coronaviruses,\216 \215other respiratory diseases,\216 and \215non-respiratory emerging pathogens,\216 allowing for a breadth of potential pandemic threats while maintaining granularity in the types of pathogens assessed. The utility of this framework, especially as it continues to incorporate more robust and topically-relevant variables, is that it is based in epidemiological modeling and captures variation at the subnational level, thus providing a complex picture of how an emerging pandemic threat may endanger countries. Another key benefit of the model is that its results are formatted for a non-epidemiological audience. While its key end-users are those in risk management, it may be well-adapted for engagements with political stakeholders aiming to quantify the economic and financial risk of an early pandemic threat. This tool also uniquely employs probabilistic modeling to project risk. However, due to the proprietary nature of the model, information on the predictive capacity of this tool is limited. This tool may only work to retroactively assess previous pandemics and characterize the probable extent of pandemics when in their early stages.10, 11, 46)/Pg 22 0 R/K[0 1]>>
endobj

