532 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/T(In 2010, the CDC released the Influenza Risk Assessment Tool \(IRAT\). This tool compiles a list of all known influenza A viruses circulating in animals that have not yet been transmitted to humans and assesses their potential pandemic risk. While the IRAT is not intended to be a predictor of future pandemics, it evaluates the potential of a pathogen to have human-to-human transmission, the possible public health effects of such a spread, and how a given pathogen may compare to others in previous IRAT reports. This tool is updated as new information arises about a given strain, with the oldest entry being in 1996, and the most recent update being in 2024. The IRAT gives each influenza A strain a set of scores that are qualitative in nature. While this tool grants scores for emergence and impact of a given pathogen across ten different indicators, these scores only provide a relative comparison of severity between strains. In addition to not having predictive capacity, the CDC itself reports that this tool is limited due to its reliance on scarce preliminary data like human infection, particularly for strains not yet seen in humans. Additional constraints are that the IRAT only tracks ten risk elements across three categories and does not incorporate geographic )/E(In 2010, the CDC released the Influenza Risk Assessment Tool \(IRAT\). This tool compiles a list of all known influenza A viruses circulating in animals that have not yet been transmitted to humans and assesses their potential pandemic risk. While the IRAT is not intended to be a predictor of future pandemics, it evaluates the potential of a pathogen to have human-to-human transmission, the possible public health effects of such a spread, and how a given pathogen may compare to others in previous IRAT reports. This tool is updated as new information arises about a given strain, with the oldest entry being in 1996, and the most recent update being in 2024. The IRAT gives each influenza A strain a set of scores that are qualitative in nature. While this tool grants scores for emergence and impact of a given pathogen across ten different indicators, these scores only provide a relative comparison of severity between strains. In addition to not having predictive capacity, the CDC itself reports that this tool is limited due to its reliance on scarce preliminary data like human infection, particularly for strains not yet seen in humans. Additional constraints are that the IRAT only tracks ten risk elements across three categories and does not incorporate geographic )/Pg 22 0 R/K[4 5]>>
endobj

