558 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/T(University\220s Pandemic Center. With 37 indicators spread across 6 categories \(prevention, detection and reporting, rapid response, health systems, compliance with international norms, and risk environment\), the GHS Index provides each assessed country with a score and an overall ranking of their health security capabilities. This benchmarking index is created using publicly available data sourced by researchers from Economist Impact. The scoring process is supported by a panel of experts, and GHS Index output is sent to countries to validate findings prior to publication.   The second edition of the GHS Index, published in 2021, was updated to include new metrics such as assessing zoonotic disease spillover events, scaling testing capabilities for known and novel pathogens, and considering misinformation and disinformation. However, a major limitation of this tool is its reliance on publicly available data, which is subject to variation in data quality as well as self-report bias. During COVID-19, the GHS Index was found to be a poor predictor of which countries were well prepared to respond to the pandemic, with researchers finding that higher GHS Index scores did not correlate with lower COVID-19 cases and fatalities.33-35, 42)/E(University\220s Pandemic Center. With 37 indicators spread across 6 categories \(prevention, detection and reporting, rapid response, health systems, compliance with international norms, and risk environment\), the GHS Index provides each assessed country with a score and an overall ranking of their health security capabilities. This benchmarking index is created using publicly available data sourced by researchers from Economist Impact. The scoring process is supported by a panel of experts, and GHS Index output is sent to countries to validate findings prior to publication.   The second edition of the GHS Index, published in 2021, was updated to include new metrics such as assessing zoonotic disease spillover events, scaling testing capabilities for known and novel pathogens, and considering misinformation and disinformation. However, a major limitation of this tool is its reliance on publicly available data, which is subject to variation in data quality as well as self-report bias. During COVID-19, the GHS Index was found to be a poor predictor of which countries were well prepared to respond to the pandemic, with researchers finding that higher GHS Index scores did not correlate with lower COVID-19 cases and fatalities.33-35, 42)/Pg 27 0 R/K[0 1 2]>>
endobj

