563 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/T(Table 2 provides a comparison of these frameworks with regard to scope, data sources, timelines, and output types. For example, some of the tools, such as the IRAT, only examine known pathogens\220 capacity for pandemic risk, while others, such as TIPRA, aim to assess the pandemic risk of novel pathogens. Other frameworks, such as the GHS Index, the GPMB, INFORM, and the JEE, provide useful assessments of state preparedness and help countries target investments toward health and pandemic-related infrastructure. However, they are not predictive of specific pathogenic threats or overall pandemic risk. )/E(Table 2 provides a comparison of these frameworks with regard to scope, data sources, timelines, and output types. For example, some of the tools, such as the IRAT, only examine known pathogens\220 capacity for pandemic risk, while others, such as TIPRA, aim to assess the pandemic risk of novel pathogens. Other frameworks, such as the GHS Index, the GPMB, INFORM, and the JEE, provide useful assessments of state preparedness and help countries target investments toward health and pandemic-related infrastructure. However, they are not predictive of specific pathogenic threats or overall pandemic risk. )/Pg 28 0 R/K[0 1]>>
endobj

564 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/T(As such, existing tools either use predictive modeling to assess risk and preparedness broadly, or provide pathogen-specific risk assessment, but none manages to synthesize all of these dimensions to provide a unified and forward-looking assessment of pandemic risk. The fact that most of the tools in this list provide qualitative assessments may indicate that, when bringing together such a vast array of indicators, it may not be feasible to produce reliable quantitative predictions. Finally, there is little documented evidence of uptake of the majority of tools by policymakers. This suggests that the tools\220 outputs may be difficult to interpret and convert into concrete policy actions that strengthen research, development, and preparedness efforts.)/E(As such, existing tools either use predictive modeling to assess risk and preparedness broadly, or provide pathogen-specific risk assessment, but none manages to synthesize all of these dimensions to provide a unified and forward-looking assessment of pandemic risk. The fact that most of the tools in this list provide qualitative assessments may indicate that, when bringing together such a vast array of indicators, it may not be feasible to produce reliable quantitative predictions. Finally, there is little documented evidence of uptake of the majority of tools by policymakers. This suggests that the tools\220 outputs may be difficult to interpret and convert into concrete policy actions that strengthen research, development, and preparedness efforts.)/Pg 28 0 R/K[2]>>
endobj

565 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/T(Along with differences in output types and purposes, these assessments vary significantly in their coverage of various pandemic risk and preparedness indicators. Figure 1 summarizes the coverage of indicators across ten of the reviewed pandemic risk assessment frameworks, mapping which dimensions of infectious disease risk, contextual factors, prevention, detection, and response are addressed within each assessment. This comparison shows that while some indicators \(such as population immunity, zoonotic risks, and core surveillance functions\) are covered across multiple assessments, other categories \(such as antimicrobial resistance, vector-borne and chemical risks, or governance factors such as financing\) appear less frequently. This distribution highlights the varying purposes of each frameworks, ranging from pathogen-specific tools \(e.g., IRAT, TIPRA, PSAF\) to preparedness indices \(e.g., GHS Index, INFORM-EPI, JEE, SPAR\) and modeling-based platforms \(e.g., CFA, GLEAM\). The table illustrates how no single PRA framework provides comprehensive coverage of both biological, contextual, and emerging determinants of pandemic risk.)/E(Along with differences in output types and purposes, these assessments vary significantly in their coverage of various pandemic risk and preparedness indicators. Figure 1 summarizes the coverage of indicators across ten of the reviewed pandemic risk assessment frameworks, mapping which dimensions of infectious disease risk, contextual factors, prevention, detection, and response are addressed within each assessment. This comparison shows that while some indicators \(such as population immunity, zoonotic risks, and core surveillance functions\) are covered across multiple assessments, other categories \(such as antimicrobial resistance, vector-borne and chemical risks, or governance factors such as financing\) appear less frequently. This distribution highlights the varying purposes of each frameworks, ranging from pathogen-specific tools \(e.g., IRAT, TIPRA, PSAF\) to preparedness indices \(e.g., GHS Index, INFORM-EPI, JEE, SPAR\) and modeling-based platforms \(e.g., CFA, GLEAM\). The table illustrates how no single PRA framework provides comprehensive coverage of both biological, contextual, and emerging determinants of pandemic risk.)/Pg 28 0 R/K[3]>>
endobj

