647 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/T(The literature review and series of expert consultations highlighted numerous gaps in existing tools and frameworks that work to evaluate pandemic preparedness, capacity, and risk. Pandemics are complex, adaptive systems with a multitude of both static and dynamic factors that should be used to predict risk\204many of the gaps we identified center around which of these variables are commonly excluded from pandemic preparedness and risk evaluations. Moreover, many of the existing frameworks and tools available to policymakers, financers, and health officials have an overrepresentation of static variables like healthcare system strength and emergency response infrastructure. They fail to account with high-enough frequency and granularity for constantly evolving, contextual factors, such as climate, migration and transportation networks, political buy-in, and public trust. Experts suggest this lack of dynamic coverage may contribute to existing models\220 inability to accurately predict a country\220s risk of and performance in a future pandemic. )/E(The literature review and series of expert consultations highlighted numerous gaps in existing tools and frameworks that work to evaluate pandemic preparedness, capacity, and risk. Pandemics are complex, adaptive systems with a multitude of both static and dynamic factors that should be used to predict risk\204many of the gaps we identified center around which of these variables are commonly excluded from pandemic preparedness and risk evaluations. Moreover, many of the existing frameworks and tools available to policymakers, financers, and health officials have an overrepresentation of static variables like healthcare system strength and emergency response infrastructure. They fail to account with high-enough frequency and granularity for constantly evolving, contextual factors, such as climate, migration and transportation networks, political buy-in, and public trust. Experts suggest this lack of dynamic coverage may contribute to existing models\220 inability to accurately predict a country\220s risk of and performance in a future pandemic. )/Pg 32 0 R/K[1 2 3]>>
endobj

648 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/E(We have broken these gaps down into four categories: political and social factors, local context, economic impacts, and the role of biosecurity, which we will explore in greater detail below.)/Pg 32 0 R/K[5]>>
endobj

649 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/T(Political and Social Factors)/E(Political and Social Factors)/Pg 32 0 R/K[4]>>
endobj

650 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/Figure/P 298 0 R/Pg 32 0 R/K[7]>>
endobj

651 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/T( Even tools such as the GHS Index that do cover such topics take a high-level, structural approach to the sociopolitical landscape, preventing them from meaningfully capturing social and political determinants of pandemic risk. )/E( Even tools such as the GHS Index that do cover such topics take a high-level, structural approach to the sociopolitical landscape, preventing them from meaningfully capturing social and political determinants of pandemic risk. )/Pg 33 0 R/K[4 5]>>
endobj

652 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/T(Local Context )/E(Local Context )/Pg 33 0 R/K[3]>>
endobj

653 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/T(Economic Impacts)/E(Economic Impacts)/Pg 33 0 R/K[6]>>
endobj

654 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/T(Existing models largely omit the economic dimensions of pandemic risk in a country. None of the models reviewed estimate the toll of pandemics on a country\220s fiscal and macroeconomic wellbeing, despite the documented, significant costs of response and recovery. Predictions of such costs could act as incentives for policymakers to invest in pandemic preparedness and response infrastructure, and many experts expressed interest in a tool that jointly measures epidemiological and economic risk of pandemics for a given country. )/E(Existing models largely omit the economic dimensions of pandemic risk in a country. None of the models reviewed estimate the toll of pandemics on a country\220s fiscal and macroeconomic wellbeing, despite the documented, significant costs of response and recovery. Predictions of such costs could act as incentives for policymakers to invest in pandemic preparedness and response infrastructure, and many experts expressed interest in a tool that jointly measures epidemiological and economic risk of pandemics for a given country. )/Pg 33 0 R/K[0 1]>>
endobj

655 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/T(Additionally, all but one of the existing tools assessed \(Moody\220s RMS-IDM\) fail to take into account how a country's economic activities\204such as reliance on certain industries, trading partners, or supply chains\204alter their pandemic risk. Countries that rely on a dominant sector may have more vulnerability to economic disruptions and therefore a limited ability to finance and execute outbreak-control measures. Additionally, economic specialization in particular sectors, such as agriculture, livestock, and wildlife trade, makes a country more likely to encounter certain pathogens which could lead to an outbreak, and therefore should be measured in order to accurately predict pandemic risk.4 )/E(Additionally, all but one of the existing tools assessed \(Moody\220s RMS-IDM\) fail to take into account how a country's economic activities\204such as reliance on certain industries, trading partners, or supply chains\204alter their pandemic risk. Countries that rely on a dominant sector may have more vulnerability to economic disruptions and therefore a limited ability to finance and execute outbreak-control measures. Additionally, economic specialization in particular sectors, such as agriculture, livestock, and wildlife trade, makes a country more likely to encounter certain pathogens which could lead to an outbreak, and therefore should be measured in order to accurately predict pandemic risk.4 )/Pg 33 0 R/K[2]>>
endobj

656 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/Figure/P 299 0 R/Pg 33 0 R/K[7]>>
endobj

657 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/T(As noted, Moody\220s RMS does take these economics factors into account, highlighting an opportunity for partnership between the global health sector and private sector entities, such as insurance companies, to assess economic factors and their relationship to pandemic risk. )/E(As noted, Moody\220s RMS does take these economics factors into account, highlighting an opportunity for partnership between the global health sector and private sector entities, such as insurance companies, to assess economic factors and their relationship to pandemic risk. )/Pg 34 0 R/K[3 4]>>
endobj

658 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/T(The Role of Biosecurity)/E(The Role of Biosecurity)/Pg 34 0 R/K[5]>>
endobj

659 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/T(Based on expert consultations and the available literature, there appears to be a divide in the global health community on whether biosecurity-related variables, such as a country\220s defensive work with dangerous pathogens or threats of offensive bioweapon release from adversaries, should be included in pandemic risk assessments. Indeed, this uncertainty extends into pandemic preparedness and capacity frameworks, with some tools \(such as the JEE and GHS Index\) including high-level variables relating to biosecurity risks and others omitting them entirely. While there is no field-wide consensus on how such factors should be integrated into existing tools and frameworks, there is clear agreement that biosecurity issues, like the ones mentioned above, are an important aspect of risk. Therefore, they merit consideration to thoroughly predict the risk of pandemic emergence. )/E(Based on expert consultations and the available literature, there appears to be a divide in the global health community on whether biosecurity-related variables, such as a country\220s defensive work with dangerous pathogens or threats of offensive bioweapon release from adversaries, should be included in pandemic risk assessments. Indeed, this uncertainty extends into pandemic preparedness and capacity frameworks, with some tools \(such as the JEE and GHS Index\) including high-level variables relating to biosecurity risks and others omitting them entirely. While there is no field-wide consensus on how such factors should be integrated into existing tools and frameworks, there is clear agreement that biosecurity issues, like the ones mentioned above, are an important aspect of risk. Therefore, they merit consideration to thoroughly predict the risk of pandemic emergence. )/Pg 34 0 R/K[0 1 2]>>
endobj

660 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/T(By including the above variables, existing frameworks and tools could produce more holistic risk assessments and would likely be better able to predict global, national, and subnational risk, preparedness, and performance capacity for future pandemics. However, including these additional variables in existing tools is technically and methodologically challenging. Some of these factors, like biosecurity and the socio-political environment of a given country, may not be captured by publicly available data, which most existing frameworks rely on. As such, private data will likely be necessary to accurately assess the current state of a country\220s risk for pandemic emergence. Additionally, refining accurate metrics of such dynamic and social indices, such as political instability and public trust will require careful development, to ensure smooth integration with more well-established metrics, and to minimize uncertainty incorporated into outputs.  )/E(By including the above variables, existing frameworks and tools could produce more holistic risk assessments and would likely be better able to predict global, national, and subnational risk, preparedness, and performance capacity for future pandemics. However, including these additional variables in existing tools is technically and methodologically challenging. Some of these factors, like biosecurity and the socio-political environment of a given country, may not be captured by publicly available data, which most existing frameworks rely on. As such, private data will likely be necessary to accurately assess the current state of a country\220s risk for pandemic emergence. Additionally, refining accurate metrics of such dynamic and social indices, such as political instability and public trust will require careful development, to ensure smooth integration with more well-established metrics, and to minimize uncertainty incorporated into outputs.  )/Pg 34 0 R/K[6 7 8]>>
endobj

661 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/T(Takeaways:)/E(Takeaways:)/Pg 34 0 R/K[9]>>
endobj

662 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/P 1434 0 R/T(Major gaps in existing frameworks include a lack of inclusion of: )/E(Major gaps in existing frameworks include a lack of inclusion of: )/Pg 34 0 R/K[10]>>
endobj

