endobj

670 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/Figure/P 301 0 R/Pg 35 0 R/K[11]>>
endobj

671 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/T(After identifying gaps in existing frameworks, we sought to understand whether existing tools are being used by intended stakeholders and whether they provide utility in the work end-users are performing day-to-day. In most cases, the current tools do not provide tangible benefit\204in part due to the gaps discussed previously, but also due to mismatches between information provided by tools and the information required by stakeholders. )/E(After identifying gaps in existing frameworks, we sought to understand whether existing tools are being used by intended stakeholders and whether they provide utility in the work end-users are performing day-to-day. In most cases, the current tools do not provide tangible benefit\204in part due to the gaps discussed previously, but also due to mismatches between information provided by tools and the information required by stakeholders. )/Pg 35 0 R/K[0 1 2]>>
endobj

672 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/E(To explore this misalignment, we have identified six categories of potential end-users, and how risk evaluation outputs can be best tailored to their workflows. We discuss each category in detail below. )/Pg 35 0 R/K[9 10]>>
endobj

673 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/T(Fiscal Policymakers)/E(Fiscal Policymakers)/Pg 35 0 R/K[8]>>
endobj

674 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/T(Sound financing is critical to pandemic preparedness and response, making the support of policymakers who allocate monetary resources and set budget priorities \(e.g. finance ministers, legislative bodies, secretaries of treasuries, etc.\) equally essential. Existing tools and frameworks do not adequately meet this group's need for concise, actionable, and contextualized information, including quantifiable evidence of impact, concrete investment opportunities, and insight into the local political economy \(such as a host country\220s debt levels\).)/E(Sound financing is critical to pandemic preparedness and response, making the support of policymakers who allocate monetary resources and set budget priorities \(e.g. finance ministers, legislative bodies, secretaries of treasuries, etc.\) equally essential. Existing tools and frameworks do not adequately meet this group's need for concise, actionable, and contextualized information, including quantifiable evidence of impact, concrete investment opportunities, and insight into the local political economy \(such as a host country\220s debt levels\).)/Pg 35 0 R/K[4 5]>>
endobj

675 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/T(With many competing priorities, fiscal policymakers need pragmatic, quantitative analyses to make financing decisions. Fiscal policymakers are constantly dealing with crises, caught between addressing current struggles and solving systemic issues at the national level; as a longer-term investment, pandemic preparedness often falls to the bottom of the priority list in times of optimal health security. )/E(With many competing priorities, fiscal policymakers need pragmatic, quantitative analyses to make financing decisions. Fiscal policymakers are constantly dealing with crises, caught between addressing current struggles and solving systemic issues at the national level; as a longer-term investment, pandemic preparedness often falls to the bottom of the priority list in times of optimal health security. )/Pg 35 0 R/K[6 7]>>
endobj

676 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/T(Current tools communicate the response capability of a country, relative to other countries\220 pandemic preparedness and international standards, rather than connecting gaps in such capabilities directly to the quantifiable, financial impacts such gaps may have domestically. This relative frame of reference in capacity, while very useful for diplomats and pandemic scientists, is situated outside the scope of a finance minister, whose focus is often on weighing various domestic issues rather than international competition. Additionally, the current format of many of these tools\220 output is neither concise enough nor impact-based to be useful in the high-intensity decision-making landscape.)/E(Current tools communicate the response capability of a country, relative to other countries\220 pandemic preparedness and international standards, rather than connecting gaps in such capabilities directly to the quantifiable, financial impacts such gaps may have domestically. This relative frame of reference in capacity, while very useful for diplomats and pandemic scientists, is situated outside the scope of a finance minister, whose focus is often on weighing various domestic issues rather than international competition. Additionally, the current format of many of these tools\220 output is neither concise enough nor impact-based to be useful in the high-intensity decision-making landscape.)/Pg 35 0 R>>
endobj

677 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/Figure/P 301 0 R/Pg 35 0 R/K[12]>>
endobj

678 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/T(Other Policymakers)/E(Other Policymakers)/Pg 36 0 R/K[3]>>
endobj

679 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H4/T(Local and high-level policymakers face a wide range of challenges when it comes to preventing and responding to pandemics. Firstly, policymakers are beholden to their constituents, as well as other branches of government, including oversight committees, advisors, and lawmakers. As such, their actions must align with the needs and desires of these parties lest they risk re-election failure or funding cuts. Secondly, most policymakers work across multiple sectors, and as such, might often choose to prioritize issues which are currently affecting their constituents and which pose the most immediate threat to the country\220s wellbeing. Lastly, policymakers are often facing competing priorities for their time. As such, they need tools which are easily digestible, topically relevant, action oriented, and capable of providing real-time insights. )/E(Local and high-level policymakers face a wide range of challenges when it comes to preventing and responding to pandemics. Firstly, policymakers are beholden to their constituents, as well as other branches of government, including oversight committees, advisors, and lawmakers. As such, their actions must align with the needs and desires of these parties lest they risk re-election failure or funding cuts. Secondly, most policymakers work across multiple sectors, and as such, might often choose to prioritize issues which are currently affecting their constituents and which pose the most immediate threat to the country\220s wellbeing. Lastly, policymakers are often facing competing priorities for their time. As such, they need tools which are easily digestible, topically relevant, action oriented, and capable of providing real-time insights. )/Pg 36 0 R/K[0 1 2]>>
endobj

680 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/T(\215If it doesn\220t interoperate and localize, it won\220t land.\216)/E(\215If it doesn\220t interoperate and localize, it won\220t land.\216)/Pg 36 0 R/K[4]>>
endobj

681 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/T( - Expert Consultant)/E( - Expert Consultant)/Pg 36 0 R/K[5]>>
endobj

682 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/Figure/P 302 0 R/Pg 36 0 R/K[6]>>
endobj

683 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/T(Policymakers must consider the needs of many different stakeholders, including their constituents, oversight committees, lawmakers, other branches of government, and advisors. Additionally, most policymakers are also juggling multiple multisectoral issues at a given time. This may lead to the over-prioritization of easy-fix issues or problems which are most pressing for their constituents or the country. However, preparing for pandemics before they occur is incredibly important for real-time response to an emerging pathogenic threat. This includes building up health infrastructure, public trust in science and in the government, and potential treatment pathways. However, not all policymakers share this understanding or are able to enact change in response to it due to competing priorities and limited budgeting. Such fluctuating political will may contribute to policymakers' lack of interest in pandemic risk tools or their non-use of existing pandemic-related tools. )/E(Policymakers must consider the needs of many different stakeholders, including their constituents, oversight committees, lawmakers, other branches of government, and advisors. Additionally, most policymakers are also juggling multiple multisectoral issues at a given time. This may lead to the over-prioritization of easy-fix issues or problems which are most pressing for their constituents or the country. However, preparing for pandemics before they occur is incredibly important for real-time response to an emerging pathogenic threat. This includes building up health infrastructure, public trust in science and in the government, and potential treatment pathways. However, not all policymakers share this understanding or are able to enact change in response to it due to competing priorities and limited budgeting. Such fluctuating political will may contribute to policymakers' lack of interest in pandemic risk tools or their non-use of existing pandemic-related tools. )/Pg 37 0 R/K[0 1]>>
endobj

684 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/T(With many voices and opinions, it may also be beyond the policymaker\220s control whether they invest in pandemics, and to what degree. Cycles of attention may influence what topics policymakers prioritize. During an epidemic or pandemic, constituent and policy-level support for controlling disease spread and improving health infrastructure is extremely high. Thus, policymakers will be incentivized to dedicate additional time and funds to combatting pandemics via increased infrastructure, scientific investments, and public messaging. However, when health threats appear low, this outpouring of public and private support may wane, forcing policymakers to dedicate time and resources to other, non-pandemic related issues. Given this dispersion of influence, it is vital that public and private support for pandemic-related investments remains high in order for any pandemic risk tool to be utilized in the policy sphere beyond pandemic-focused subgroups.)/E(With many voices and opinions, it may also be beyond the policymaker\220s control whether they invest in pandemics, and to what degree. Cycles of attention may influence what topics policymakers prioritize. During an epidemic or pandemic, constituent and policy-level support for controlling disease spread and improving health infrastructure is extremely high. Thus, policymakers will be incentivized to dedicate additional time and funds to combatting pandemics via increased infrastructure, scientific investments, and public messaging. However, when health threats appear low, this outpouring of public and private support may wane, forcing policymakers to dedicate time and resources to other, non-pandemic related issues. Given this dispersion of influence, it is vital that public and private support for pandemic-related investments remains high in order for any pandemic risk tool to be utilized in the policy sphere beyond pandemic-focused subgroups.)/Pg 37 0 R>>
endobj

685 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/T(Policymakers are time-limited and often face an overwhelming amount of information from various sources. They may not have the time to explore lengthy, multilayered tools or to parse dense technical language. Any pandemic-related tool must understand this limited capacity and produce results which are easy to quickly comprehend, clearly highlight key takeaways, are topically relevant to a policymaker\220s sphere of influence and expertise, and provide real-time, actionable insights. Expert consultations clarified that policymakers were interested in tools which provided them clear and concise takeaways which they could then feasibly use to implement new practices, laws, or even grants to improve their pandemic-related infrastructure and health landscape. )/E(Policymakers are time-limited and often face an overwhelming amount of information from various sources. They may not have the time to explore lengthy, multilayered tools or to parse dense technical language. Any pandemic-related tool must understand this limited capacity and produce results which are easy to quickly comprehend, clearly highlight key takeaways, are topically relevant to a policymaker\220s sphere of influence and expertise, and provide real-time, actionable insights. Expert consultations clarified that policymakers were interested in tools which provided them clear and concise takeaways which they could then feasibly use to implement new practices, laws, or even grants to improve their pandemic-related infrastructure and health landscape. )/Pg 37 0 R/K[2 3 4]>>
endobj

686 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/Figure/P 303 0 R/Pg 37 0 R/K[5]>>
endobj

687 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/Figure/P 303 0 R/Pg 37 0 R/K[6]>>
endobj

688 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/T(Local and Regional Health Officials)/E(Local and Regional Health Officials)/Pg 38 0 R/K[4]>>
endobj

689 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/T(Local and regional health officials require targeted, localized information on pandemic risk and preparedness in order to better understand differential risk and capacity and the local level. However, most tools operate at the national level and therefore do not take into account local diversity, community makeup, or divergence in healthcare system strength across urban and rural areas. While these broader takeaways on capacity or risk are helpful to local and regional health officials, they often do not provide enough granularity to properly guide investments and outreach. Similarly, they often cannot recognize risk factors specific to local contexts, such as unique agricultural processes, hygiene customs, or community health infrastructure. Expert consultants interviewed expressed an interest in quantitative pandemic tools which are able to measure preparedness, capacity, and risk in local and subnational contexts, as opposed to national ones. )/E(Local and regional health officials require targeted, localized information on pandemic risk and preparedness in order to better understand differential risk and capacity and the local level. However, most tools operate at the national level and therefore do not take into account local diversity, community makeup, or divergence in healthcare system strength across urban and rural areas. While these broader takeaways on capacity or risk are helpful to local and regional health officials, they often do not provide enough granularity to properly guide investments and outreach. Similarly, they often cannot recognize risk factors specific to local contexts, such as unique agricultural processes, hygiene customs, or community health infrastructure. Expert consultants interviewed expressed an interest in quantitative pandemic tools which are able to measure preparedness, capacity, and risk in local and subnational contexts, as opposed to national ones. )/Pg 38 0 R/K[0 1]>>
endobj

690 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/T(Additionally, officials highlighted the need for actionable intelligence which directly measures risks and provides key takeaways to guide investments in a given area. However, unlike policymakers, who explicitly fund national-level programs, these investments will likely take the form of local preparedness paradigms, staffing changes, community vaccination and treatment infrastructure, and professional development for local healthcare staff. )/E(Additionally, officials highlighted the need for actionable intelligence which directly measures risks and provides key takeaways to guide investments in a given area. However, unlike policymakers, who explicitly fund national-level programs, these investments will likely take the form of local preparedness paradigms, staffing changes, community vaccination and treatment infrastructure, and professional development for local healthcare staff. )/Pg 38 0 R/K[2 3]>>
endobj

691 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/T(Compared to other end-users, these health officials are better equipped to utilize tools which provide more technical, epidemiologically-oriented outputs, as they often have deep expertise in these areas. In addition, unlike policymakers, who are overwhelmed with information and thus need only key takeaways, local and regional health officials often need a wide breadth and depth of data, especially that which relates to epidemic-level pathogenic threats to their constituents.)/E(Compared to other end-users, these health officials are better equipped to utilize tools which provide more technical, epidemiologically-oriented outputs, as they often have deep expertise in these areas. In addition, unlike policymakers, who are overwhelmed with information and thus need only key takeaways, local and regional health officials often need a wide breadth and depth of data, especially that which relates to epidemic-level pathogenic threats to their constituents.)/Pg 38 0 R>>
endobj

692 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/T(\215Career civil servants are poorly equipped to deal with [pandemic policy] unless they have real technical expertise.\216 - Expert Consultant)/E(\215Career civil servants are poorly equipped to deal with [pandemic policy] unless they have real technical expertise.\216 - Expert Consultant)/Pg 38 0 R/K[5]>>
endobj

693 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/T(Lastly, unlike some other end-users who exhibit an oscillating will to invest in pandemic preparedness, local health officials tend to remain consistently motivated to improve health outcomes and combat pandemic emergence. As such, any pandemic-related tool which provides actionable insight will likely be of interest to this group of end-users, and can actively assist them in bettering their communities and preventing the next pandemic\204as long as it is designed for subnational specificity. )/E(Lastly, unlike some other end-users who exhibit an oscillating will to invest in pandemic preparedness, local health officials tend to remain consistently motivated to improve health outcomes and combat pandemic emergence. As such, any pandemic-related tool which provides actionable insight will likely be of interest to this group of end-users, and can actively assist them in bettering their communities and preventing the next pandemic\204as long as it is designed for subnational specificity. )/Pg 38 0 R/K[6 7]>>
endobj

694 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/Figure/P 304 0 R/Pg 38 0 R/K[8]>>
endobj

695 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/T(Philanthropic and Funding Organizations)/E(Philanthropic and Funding Organizations)/Pg 39 0 R/K[4]>>
endobj

696 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/T(Like fiscal policymakers, charitable foundations and donors are primarily motivated by the potential impact and return on their investments. However, as mentioned, existing frameworks do not predict the broader economic impact of pandemic risk and preparedness efforts. Current models of the averted costs of a potential pandemic do not neatly fit into traditional return-on-investment metrics, as they must factor in lost labor and varied, fundamentally counterfactual scenarios. Nonetheless, many philanthropic stakeholders suggested that knowing the impact of the money they spend is essential for making their financing decisions. )/E(Like fiscal policymakers, charitable foundations and donors are primarily motivated by the potential impact and return on their investments. However, as mentioned, existing frameworks do not predict the broader economic impact of pandemic risk and preparedness efforts. Current models of the averted costs of a potential pandemic do not neatly fit into traditional return-on-investment metrics, as they must factor in lost labor and varied, fundamentally counterfactual scenarios. Nonetheless, many philanthropic stakeholders suggested that knowing the impact of the money they spend is essential for making their financing decisions. )/Pg 39 0 R/K[0 1]>>
endobj

697 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/T(Discussions with current pandemic financing leaders revealed that there are dedicated funds already in place for emergency and outbreak response. Organizations that house these funds include multilateral groups such as the Pandemic Fund and the World Bank, as well as broad philanthropic organizations that allocate portions of their funding to pandemics such as the Gates Foundation, as well as smaller foundations, such as PAX sapiens. )/E(Discussions with current pandemic financing leaders revealed that there are dedicated funds already in place for emergency and outbreak response. Organizations that house these funds include multilateral groups such as the Pandemic Fund and the World Bank, as well as broad philanthropic organizations that allocate portions of their funding to pandemics such as the Gates Foundation, as well as smaller foundations, such as PAX sapiens. )/Pg 39 0 R/K[2 3]>>
endobj

698 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/T(Philanthropic organizations are often assumed to have a key advantage over policymakers; because their budgets are not tied to political cycles, they should be well-positioned to make far-sighted investments, such as those meant to further pandemic preparedness. Yet, discussions with experts in this realm suggest that this is not the case. Leading organizations, such as the Wellcome Trust, tend to set aside funds targeted for specific purposes, usually to scale up research related to outbreak response against known pathogens. This programmatic focus leaves relatively little room for the kinds of speculative investments that a forward-looking PRA requires, such as identifying potential novel pathogens that do not have any outbreak history or diseases that exist in animals but have not yet spilled over to humans yet. In this way, philanthropic donors that could counteract political short-sightedness end up mirroring it. )/E(Philanthropic organizations are often assumed to have a key advantage over policymakers; because their budgets are not tied to political cycles, they should be well-positioned to make far-sighted investments, such as those meant to further pandemic preparedness. Yet, discussions with experts in this realm suggest that this is not the case. Leading organizations, such as the Wellcome Trust, tend to set aside funds targeted for specific purposes, usually to scale up research related to outbreak response against known pathogens. This programmatic focus leaves relatively little room for the kinds of speculative investments that a forward-looking PRA requires, such as identifying potential novel pathogens that do not have any outbreak history or diseases that exist in animals but have not yet spilled over to humans yet. In this way, philanthropic donors that could counteract political short-sightedness end up mirroring it. )/Pg 39 0 R/K[5 6]>>
endobj

699 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/T(\215Donors are often running their projects in silos.\216 - Expert Consultant)/E(\215Donors are often running their projects in silos.\216 - Expert Consultant)/Pg 39 0 R/K[7]>>
endobj

700 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/T(\215After every surge comes neglect. PRA has to be built to survive the quiet years.\216 - Expert Consultant)/E(\215After every surge comes neglect. PRA has to be built to survive the quiet years.\216 - Expert Consultant)/Pg 39 0 R/K[8]>>
endobj

701 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/Figure/P 305 0 R/Pg 39 0 R/K[9]>>
endobj

702 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/T(Given this earmarking of funds for targeted purposes, it is unsurprising that PRA and prevention efforts receive less funding attention than emergency response and preparedness. Detecting risk often requires broader efforts that probe at the unknown, which is the opposite of what funders prefer to target\204known projects they know will make an impact. Current benchmarking tools reinforce this bias toward the measurable and immediate, feeding into the lack of forward-looking, predictive models. Yet, scenario modeling is important for both policymakers and funders, especially in the realm of uncertain investments. Having reliable models of how certain policies or research projects will reduce pandemic risk is crucial for engaging philanthropic end-users and justifying investment in upstream prevention. )/E(Given this earmarking of funds for targeted purposes, it is unsurprising that PRA and prevention efforts receive less funding attention than emergency response and preparedness. Detecting risk often requires broader efforts that probe at the unknown, which is the opposite of what funders prefer to target\204known projects they know will make an impact. Current benchmarking tools reinforce this bias toward the measurable and immediate, feeding into the lack of forward-looking, predictive models. Yet, scenario modeling is important for both policymakers and funders, especially in the realm of uncertain investments. Having reliable models of how certain policies or research projects will reduce pandemic risk is crucial for engaging philanthropic end-users and justifying investment in upstream prevention. )/Pg 40 0 R/K[1 2 3]>>
endobj

703 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/T(Multilateral Organizations)/E(Multilateral Organizations)/Pg 40 0 R/K[0]>>
endobj

704 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/T(\215To shift budgets, pair risk with returns: resilience and defense speak to today\220s priorities.\216 - Expert Consultant)/E(\215To shift budgets, pair risk with returns: resilience and defense speak to today\220s priorities.\216 - Expert Consultant)/Pg 40 0 R/K[4]>>
endobj

705 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/Figure/P 306 0 R/Pg 40 0 R/K[5]>>
