706 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/T(Academia and Research Organizations)/E(Academia and Research Organizations)/Pg 41 0 R/K[4]>>
endobj

707 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/T(Academics and researchers find themselves in a unique position when it comes to pandemic preparedness and risk benchmarking tools, as they are often either the developers of these tools or have little interest in engaging with tools developed for and by other organizations. Meetings with these stakeholders reveal that they tend to focus on mechanistic understandings of disease, which existing tools do not assess with enough detail for them to properly investigate their question of interest. Very few of the stakeholders we interviewed used any tools beside the JEE to inform their work. )/E(Academics and researchers find themselves in a unique position when it comes to pandemic preparedness and risk benchmarking tools, as they are often either the developers of these tools or have little interest in engaging with tools developed for and by other organizations. Meetings with these stakeholders reveal that they tend to focus on mechanistic understandings of disease, which existing tools do not assess with enough detail for them to properly investigate their question of interest. Very few of the stakeholders we interviewed used any tools beside the JEE to inform their work. )/Pg 41 0 R/K[0 1 2]>>
endobj

708 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/T(Furthermore, some stakeholders even believe that academic researchers should stay out of policy entirely, and as such, should not be end-users of PRA-type tools. From their perspective, academics should publish their work and allow those with a stronger communication skillset to translate the scientific message into one fit for policymakers. There are many gaps in the tools that academics have developed, and it is in this realm that academics themselves require more targeted outputs from pandemic benchmarking tools for them to be useful. While academia largely remains on the back-end development of PRA and benchmarking tools, it is important not to neglect academia as a potential end-user, as they are crucial to providing pandemic expertise to governmental and multilateral organizations.)/E(Furthermore, some stakeholders even believe that academic researchers should stay out of policy entirely, and as such, should not be end-users of PRA-type tools. From their perspective, academics should publish their work and allow those with a stronger communication skillset to translate the scientific message into one fit for policymakers. There are many gaps in the tools that academics have developed, and it is in this realm that academics themselves require more targeted outputs from pandemic benchmarking tools for them to be useful. While academia largely remains on the back-end development of PRA and benchmarking tools, it is important not to neglect academia as a potential end-user, as they are crucial to providing pandemic expertise to governmental and multilateral organizations.)/Pg 41 0 R/K[3]>>
endobj

