endobj

753 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/T(Clarity of Purpose)/E(Clarity of Purpose)/Pg 45 0 R/K[2]>>
endobj

754 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/T(It must be clear what the purpose of any given tool is in order for it to be used, perceived, and communicated appropriately. We have found that preparedness tools are often perceived to have \215failed\216 because users are expecting an assessment of risk or performance during a health crisis, instead of as a diagnostic tool for preparedness. To build trust in tools and maximize their effectiveness, we suggest that tools under a PRA framework are clearly defined in their purpose to provide predictive value in areas such as geographic spread, economic disruptions, and health impacts. )/E(It must be clear what the purpose of any given tool is in order for it to be used, perceived, and communicated appropriately. We have found that preparedness tools are often perceived to have \215failed\216 because users are expecting an assessment of risk or performance during a health crisis, instead of as a diagnostic tool for preparedness. To build trust in tools and maximize their effectiveness, we suggest that tools under a PRA framework are clearly defined in their purpose to provide predictive value in areas such as geographic spread, economic disruptions, and health impacts. )/Pg 45 0 R/K[0 1]>>
endobj

755 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/T(2.)/E(2.)/Pg 45 0 R/K[6]>>
endobj

756 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/T(Data Governance)/E(Data Governance)/Pg 45 0 R/K[5]>>
endobj

757 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/T(Any modeling tool should be informed by high-quality and abundant data inputs. Tool owners and developers must formulate clear methods and standards for collecting, using, and sharing data. Structures for surveillance \(human, animal, environment\) and public availability of input and output data must be established.)/E(Any modeling tool should be informed by high-quality and abundant data inputs. Tool owners and developers must formulate clear methods and standards for collecting, using, and sharing data. Structures for surveillance \(human, animal, environment\) and public availability of input and output data must be established.)/Pg 45 0 R/K[4]>>
endobj

758 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/T(3. )/E(3. )/Pg 45 0 R/K[10]>>
endobj

759 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/T(Transparency of Methods)/E(Transparency of Methods)/Pg 45 0 R/K[9]>>
endobj

760 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/T(It must be clear what the purpose of any given tool is in order for it to be used, perceived, and communicated appropriately. We have found that preparedness tools are often perceived to have \215failed\216 because users are expecting an assessment of risk or performance during a health crisis, instead of as a diagnostic tool for preparedness. To build trust in tools and maximize their effectiveness, we suggest that tools under a PRA framework are clearly defined in their purpose to provide predictive value in areas such as geographic spread, economic disruptions, and health impacts. )/E(It must be clear what the purpose of any given tool is in order for it to be used, perceived, and communicated appropriately. We have found that preparedness tools are often perceived to have \215failed\216 because users are expecting an assessment of risk or performance during a health crisis, instead of as a diagnostic tool for preparedness. To build trust in tools and maximize their effectiveness, we suggest that tools under a PRA framework are clearly defined in their purpose to provide predictive value in areas such as geographic spread, economic disruptions, and health impacts. )/Pg 45 0 R/K[7 8]>>
endobj

761 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/T(4. )/E(4. )/Pg 45 0 R/K[13]>>
endobj

762 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/T(Ownership Structure)/E(Ownership Structure)/Pg 45 0 R/K[12]>>
endobj

763 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/T(Rather than housing various assessment tools under distinct institutions, we suggest a mix of country-level and independent ownership. Multilateral institutions excel at housing tools under independent leadership, but national and subnational stakeholders often have little incentive to partake in the improvement or use of these tools. Diverse ownership can maximize legitimacy and engagement.)/E(Rather than housing various assessment tools under distinct institutions, we suggest a mix of country-level and independent ownership. Multilateral institutions excel at housing tools under independent leadership, but national and subnational stakeholders often have little incentive to partake in the improvement or use of these tools. Diverse ownership can maximize legitimacy and engagement.)/Pg 45 0 R/K[11]>>
endobj

764 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/Figure/P 311 0 R/Pg 45 0 R/K[15]>>
endobj

765 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/T(Design - Architectural & Operational Principles)/E(Design - Architectural & Operational Principles)/Pg 46 0 R/K[0]>>
endobj

766 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/T(5.)/E(5.)/Pg 46 0 R/K[3]>>
endobj

767 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/T(Inclusion of Emerging Risks)/E(Inclusion of Emerging Risks)/Pg 46 0 R/K[2]>>
endobj

768 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/T(Risk assessment models should include emerging risks to ensure tools are comprehensive, adaptable, and responsive. Anthropogenic and technological drivers are some of the most significant factors of increasing pandemic threat. Additional emerging risk factors include, but are not limited to climate change, biodiversity loss and urbanization, global interconnectedness, and artificial intelligence. Integrating these drivers ensures that a tool will evolve with rapidly-changing global risk landscapes.)/E(Risk assessment models should include emerging risks to ensure tools are comprehensive, adaptable, and responsive. Anthropogenic and technological drivers are some of the most significant factors of increasing pandemic threat. Additional emerging risk factors include, but are not limited to climate change, biodiversity loss and urbanization, global interconnectedness, and artificial intelligence. Integrating these drivers ensures that a tool will evolve with rapidly-changing global risk landscapes.)/Pg 46 0 R/K[1]>>
endobj

769 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/T(6.)/E(6.)/Pg 46 0 R/K[6]>>
endobj

770 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/T(Consideration of Political and Social Context)/E(Consideration of Political and Social Context)/Pg 46 0 R/K[5]>>
endobj

771 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/T(Models run the risk of over-simplification by failing to include dynamic, environmental factors that indirectly affect outputs and predictions. With this pitfall, a model may lack real-world transferability. Specifically, factors within the political and social climate \(e.g. trust, political will, misinformation or disinformation\) have a large influence on countries\220 pandemic risk, though are difficult to measure. As such, tools should integrate granular socio-political factors to provide more accurate assessments, and refine methods of measuring such factors.)/E(Models run the risk of over-simplification by failing to include dynamic, environmental factors that indirectly affect outputs and predictions. With this pitfall, a model may lack real-world transferability. Specifically, factors within the political and social climate \(e.g. trust, political will, misinformation or disinformation\) have a large influence on countries\220 pandemic risk, though are difficult to measure. As such, tools should integrate granular socio-political factors to provide more accurate assessments, and refine methods of measuring such factors.)/Pg 46 0 R/K[4]>>
endobj

772 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/T(7.)/E(7.)/Pg 46 0 R/K[10]>>
endobj

773 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/T(Real Time Availability and Dynamic Updates)/E(Real Time Availability and Dynamic Updates)/Pg 46 0 R/K[9]>>
endobj

774 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/T(Key epidemiological indicators\204such as prevalent pathogen strains, transmission rates, number of cases, and mortality rates\204can change rapidly. Frequent, ideally real-time, updates are crucial for effective decision-making. We suggest automation, especially for models that include dynamic factors, instead of static probabilities which are often outdated, and thus can be unreliable indicators of pandemic risk. )/E(Key epidemiological indicators\204such as prevalent pathogen strains, transmission rates, number of cases, and mortality rates\204can change rapidly. Frequent, ideally real-time, updates are crucial for effective decision-making. We suggest automation, especially for models that include dynamic factors, instead of static probabilities which are often outdated, and thus can be unreliable indicators of pandemic risk. )/Pg 46 0 R/K[7 8]>>
endobj

775 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/T(8.)/E(8.)/Pg 46 0 R/K[13]>>
endobj

776 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/T(Quantify and Communicate Uncertainty)/E(Quantify and Communicate Uncertainty)/Pg 46 0 R/K[12]>>
endobj

777 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/T(PRA tools should clearly communicate how reliable their estimates are. Decision-makers require transparency about gaps and confidence in the outputs they will be using. Clear communication of the certainty of risk assessments through ranges, confidence levels, or qualitative ratings, alongside stated assumptions, will aid decision-makers in judging how much weight to place on those outputs.)/E(PRA tools should clearly communicate how reliable their estimates are. Decision-makers require transparency about gaps and confidence in the outputs they will be using. Clear communication of the certainty of risk assessments through ranges, confidence levels, or qualitative ratings, alongside stated assumptions, will aid decision-makers in judging how much weight to place on those outputs.)/Pg 46 0 R/K[11]>>
endobj

778 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/Figure/P 312 0 R/Pg 46 0 R/K[14]>>
endobj

779 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/T(9.)/E(9.)/Pg 47 0 R/K[3]>>
endobj

780 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/T(Flexibility from Global to Subnational Insights)/E(Flexibility from Global to Subnational Insights)/Pg 47 0 R/K[2]>>
endobj

781 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/T(Risk assessment models should include emerging risks to ensure tools are comprehensive, adaptable, and responsive. Anthropogenic and technological drivers are some of the most significant factors of increasing pandemic threat. Additional emerging risk factors include, but are not limited to climate change, biodiversity loss and urbanization, global interconnectedness, and artificial intelligence. Integrating these drivers ensures that a tool will evolve with rapidly-changing global risk landscapes.)/E(Risk assessment models should include emerging risks to ensure tools are comprehensive, adaptable, and responsive. Anthropogenic and technological drivers are some of the most significant factors of increasing pandemic threat. Additional emerging risk factors include, but are not limited to climate change, biodiversity loss and urbanization, global interconnectedness, and artificial intelligence. Integrating these drivers ensures that a tool will evolve with rapidly-changing global risk landscapes.)/Pg 47 0 R/K[1]>>
endobj

