endobj

800 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/T(From policymakers to philanthropic organizations, most of the potential end-users of these tools are accustomed to making high-stress decisions on topics without all the necessary information or scientific background. As a result, these reports should not be overly granular and technical, but should instead communicate actionable steps to increase pandemic preparedness or capture pandemic risk concisely, weaving in any critical background information relating to public health, vaccinology, or pathogen behavior. )/E(From policymakers to philanthropic organizations, most of the potential end-users of these tools are accustomed to making high-stress decisions on topics without all the necessary information or scientific background. As a result, these reports should not be overly granular and technical, but should instead communicate actionable steps to increase pandemic preparedness or capture pandemic risk concisely, weaving in any critical background information relating to public health, vaccinology, or pathogen behavior. )/Pg 48 0 R/K[3 4 5]>>
endobj

801 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/T(Notable exceptions to this more high-level output include end-users such as academics and local public health officials, who are equipped to engage with highly technical measures relating to pandemic capacity and risk, and who likely desire increasingly detailed technical data. However, for other policymakers, generating interest in investing in pandemics is contingent on concise, action-oriented, accessible reports.)/E(Notable exceptions to this more high-level output include end-users such as academics and local public health officials, who are equipped to engage with highly technical measures relating to pandemic capacity and risk, and who likely desire increasingly detailed technical data. However, for other policymakers, generating interest in investing in pandemics is contingent on concise, action-oriented, accessible reports.)/Pg 48 0 R>>
endobj

802 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/Figure/P 314 0 R/Pg 48 0 R/K[12]>>
endobj

803 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/T(Context-Aware Recommendations)/E(Context-Aware Recommendations)/Pg 49 0 R/K[6]>>
endobj

804 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/T(In order to tie pandemic preparedness tools to tangible progress, the products of these tools must fully contextualize all recommendations. The end-users discussed previously, especially domestic actors such as policymakers, often face constraints beyond insufficient information, such as political or financial limitations which prevent them from fully implementing the ambitious goals outlined by current tools. Incorporating an understanding of the local political economy of the country could help these tools suggest more reasonable steps for improvement. Local health officials similarly require politically and resource aware recommendations to properly identify local investments, like the re-organizations of health resources, while remaining maximally sensitive to sociopolitical and logistical constraints. As such, carefully considering contextual constraints when developing a PRA tool could lead to more feasible policy enactment and a corresponding increased openness from host countries to further international collaboration on pandemic-related initiatives.)/E(In order to tie pandemic preparedness tools to tangible progress, the products of these tools must fully contextualize all recommendations. The end-users discussed previously, especially domestic actors such as policymakers, often face constraints beyond insufficient information, such as political or financial limitations which prevent them from fully implementing the ambitious goals outlined by current tools. Incorporating an understanding of the local political economy of the country could help these tools suggest more reasonable steps for improvement. Local health officials similarly require politically and resource aware recommendations to properly identify local investments, like the re-organizations of health resources, while remaining maximally sensitive to sociopolitical and logistical constraints. As such, carefully considering contextual constraints when developing a PRA tool could lead to more feasible policy enactment and a corresponding increased openness from host countries to further international collaboration on pandemic-related initiatives.)/Pg 49 0 R/K[0 1]>>
endobj

805 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/T(Delivering Impact-Focused and Quantified Feedback)/E(Delivering Impact-Focused and Quantified Feedback)/Pg 49 0 R/K[7]>>
endobj

806 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/T(To better engage with fiscal policymakers and other economically-motivated end users, pandemic risk assessment tools should prioritize quantifying the impact of the investments they are proposing, both in terms of economics and health outcomes. Any new investment must correspond with a decrease in funding for a different sector. Thus, understanding the impact of investment, potentially even at the lives saved per dollar level, is necessary for finance-minded end users to make these decisions. PRA tools have an excellent opportunity to fill this niche. Sector-level marginal benefit estimation, which estimates the impact of a single dollar when invested in various pandemic preparedness areas, further allows for budget optimization. This empowers countries to improve their preparedness by simply re-organizing already-allocated resources more efficiently. A data-backed emphasis on impact therefore best serves fiscal policymakers and other policymakers, and encourages future efforts to improve pandemic preparedness. )/E(To better engage with fiscal policymakers and other economically-motivated end users, pandemic risk assessment tools should prioritize quantifying the impact of the investments they are proposing, both in terms of economics and health outcomes. Any new investment must correspond with a decrease in funding for a different sector. Thus, understanding the impact of investment, potentially even at the lives saved per dollar level, is necessary for finance-minded end users to make these decisions. PRA tools have an excellent opportunity to fill this niche. Sector-level marginal benefit estimation, which estimates the impact of a single dollar when invested in various pandemic preparedness areas, further allows for budget optimization. This empowers countries to improve their preparedness by simply re-organizing already-allocated resources more efficiently. A data-backed emphasis on impact therefore best serves fiscal policymakers and other policymakers, and encourages future efforts to improve pandemic preparedness. )/Pg 49 0 R/K[2 3 4]>>
endobj

807 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/T(The benefit of quantified feedback extends to philanthropic organizations and multilateral organizations as well, offering concrete justification for funding decisions being made. For local health officials as well, quantifying impacts, simply in terms of additional local statistics, would help to inform programmatic decisions as well as justify funding decisions in often delicate political and economic environments. In conclusion, quantifiable specificity is critical for every stakeholder\220s use of PRA tools to inform decisions and bring hesitant parties on board.)/E(The benefit of quantified feedback extends to philanthropic organizations and multilateral organizations as well, offering concrete justification for funding decisions being made. For local health officials as well, quantifying impacts, simply in terms of additional local statistics, would help to inform programmatic decisions as well as justify funding decisions in often delicate political and economic environments. In conclusion, quantifiable specificity is critical for every stakeholder\220s use of PRA tools to inform decisions and bring hesitant parties on board.)/Pg 49 0 R/K[5]>>
endobj

