endobj

815 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/T(\215The US is a self-sabotaging outlier on climate and health policy.\216 - Expert Consultant)/E(\215The US is a self-sabotaging outlier on climate and health policy.\216 - Expert Consultant)/Pg 51 0 R/K[0]>>
endobj

816 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/Figure/P 317 0 R/Pg 51 0 R/K[1]>>
endobj

817 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/T(Taken together, these mechanisms indicate that anthropogenic environmental change and climate change are not peripheral contextual factors but essential determinants of pandemic risk. As such, a comprehensive PRA should incorporate them as a core component of hazard evaluation. For example, it could pair viral surveillance methods with land-use and biodiversity monitoring to track how risk evolves alongside ecosystems. Regional modules could incorporate extreme weather projections and temperature, humidity, and precipitation estimates to track heightened risk scenarios. In areas with significant and growing urban density, population growth patterns could be helpful as indicators for potential high-transmission localizations. These design strategies, among others, will require collaboration between climate scientists, ecologists, and public health experts to ensure that anthropogenic environmental change factors are effectively incorporated into a PRA tool as a fundamental component of risk evaluation. )/E(Taken together, these mechanisms indicate that anthropogenic environmental change and climate change are not peripheral contextual factors but essential determinants of pandemic risk. As such, a comprehensive PRA should incorporate them as a core component of hazard evaluation. For example, it could pair viral surveillance methods with land-use and biodiversity monitoring to track how risk evolves alongside ecosystems. Regional modules could incorporate extreme weather projections and temperature, humidity, and precipitation estimates to track heightened risk scenarios. In areas with significant and growing urban density, population growth patterns could be helpful as indicators for potential high-transmission localizations. These design strategies, among others, will require collaboration between climate scientists, ecologists, and public health experts to ensure that anthropogenic environmental change factors are effectively incorporated into a PRA tool as a fundamental component of risk evaluation. )/Pg 52 0 R/K[0 1 2]>>
endobj

818 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/T(Artificial Intelligence Reducing Pandemic Risk)/E(Artificial Intelligence Reducing Pandemic Risk)/Pg 52 0 R/K[8]>>
endobj

819 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/T(Properly governed, artificial intelligence \(AI\) may improve global resilience by shortening reaction times and optimizing intervention strategies. For example, large language modeling and AI tools \(e.g., GLEAM-AI, in development\) may be able to more efficiently provide updates to models or output areas of increasing risk that may not have been otherwise considered. This also includes second-order pandemic effects such as worsened antimicrobial resistance. )/E(Properly governed, artificial intelligence \(AI\) may improve global resilience by shortening reaction times and optimizing intervention strategies. For example, large language modeling and AI tools \(e.g., GLEAM-AI, in development\) may be able to more efficiently provide updates to models or output areas of increasing risk that may not have been otherwise considered. This also includes second-order pandemic effects such as worsened antimicrobial resistance. )/Pg 52 0 R/K[3 4]>>
endobj

820 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/T(AI also makes it possible to detect pandemic-relevant health signals in near real time across the world. Through information-sharing partnerships with private sector stakeholders, such as phone and internet providers, international organizations may be able to receive real-time signals of a potential pandemic happening in a given area. For example, higher rates of worship center attendance in a community with limited medical access might indicate rising infection rates, or a localized spike in online searches for certain symptoms could reveal a developing outbreak. These alerts can come from areas where authorities are unwilling or unable to share information promptly and transparently, and can provide assessments even before local governments recognize or respond to the threat. )/E(AI also makes it possible to detect pandemic-relevant health signals in near real time across the world. Through information-sharing partnerships with private sector stakeholders, such as phone and internet providers, international organizations may be able to receive real-time signals of a potential pandemic happening in a given area. For example, higher rates of worship center attendance in a community with limited medical access might indicate rising infection rates, or a localized spike in online searches for certain symptoms could reveal a developing outbreak. These alerts can come from areas where authorities are unwilling or unable to share information promptly and transparently, and can provide assessments even before local governments recognize or respond to the threat. )/Pg 52 0 R/K[5 6]>>
endobj

821 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/T(Beyond early detection, AI can help estimate the overall level of pandemic susceptibility for each country by analyzing movement and development indicators. Pandemic risk can be correlated with complex, high-volume data such as the movement frequency of people and goods between countries, including flights, shipping routes, and trade, as well as the strength of national healthcare systems. For example, metrics like the number of hospital beds and medical staff relative to population size in each region can help predict how severely a potential outbreak might affect a region. By incorporating these indicators into risk evaluations, AI would enable governments or international organizations to plan more effectively and direct aid to regions most in need of external support.)/E(Beyond early detection, AI can help estimate the overall level of pandemic susceptibility for each country by analyzing movement and development indicators. Pandemic risk can be correlated with complex, high-volume data such as the movement frequency of people and goods between countries, including flights, shipping routes, and trade, as well as the strength of national healthcare systems. For example, metrics like the number of hospital beds and medical staff relative to population size in each region can help predict how severely a potential outbreak might affect a region. By incorporating these indicators into risk evaluations, AI would enable governments or international organizations to plan more effectively and direct aid to regions most in need of external support.)/Pg 52 0 R/K[7]>>
endobj

822 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/Figure/P 318 0 R/Pg 52 0 R/K[9]>>
endobj

823 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/E(It is important to note that the current scientific research on using AI to help reduce pandemic risk is minimal, so the application of AI and limitations would likely require further research before applications can begin.)/Pg 53 0 R/K[0]>>
endobj

824 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/E(Artificial Intelligence Increasing Pandemic Risk)/Pg 53 0 R/K[1]>>
endobj

825 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/E(At the same time, AI has the capacity to increase pandemic risk by undermining social cohesion through amplified misinformation, lowering barriers to planning and executing biological work, and accelerating bio-design and automated experimentation.)/Pg 53 0 R/K[2]>>
endobj

