829 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/T(Although we have focused largely on the current landscape of pandemic-related tools and their gaps, alongside engagement strategies for key stakeholders, there are factors external to the tool itself that may impact its ability to operate properly in the global sphere or produce meaningful and actionable output. These factors go beyond the tool\220s design, variables, and output type, and instead are centered around its operating environment: governments, their relationship with civil society, and more general social cohesion dynamics. A PRA tool should, like many of the existing frameworks, take these external factors into account. )/E(Although we have focused largely on the current landscape of pandemic-related tools and their gaps, alongside engagement strategies for key stakeholders, there are factors external to the tool itself that may impact its ability to operate properly in the global sphere or produce meaningful and actionable output. These factors go beyond the tool\220s design, variables, and output type, and instead are centered around its operating environment: governments, their relationship with civil society, and more general social cohesion dynamics. A PRA tool should, like many of the existing frameworks, take these external factors into account. )/Pg 54 0 R/K[0 1 2]>>
endobj

830 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/T(One such example of an external consideration is the mis- and disinformation landscape of a given nation and the world at-large. As noted in the previous section, no matter how adept a tool is at modeling pandemic risk or identifying health system response gaps to a potential emerging pathogen, if there is a strong mis- or disinformation culture, then improvements or changes to the nation\220s healthcare system \(whether preparatory or responsive\) may be negatively received. Even worse, when an outbreak does occur, there may not be enough trust in government or in its science to convince the public that a health emergency is actually occurring or that a vaccine is both safe and necessary to prevent further disease spread. Many experts highlight that the prevalence of social media and AI in modern society and communications has heightened the threat of misinformation and has contributed to an overall lack of trust in government, especially on health-related issues. )/E(One such example of an external consideration is the mis- and disinformation landscape of a given nation and the world at-large. As noted in the previous section, no matter how adept a tool is at modeling pandemic risk or identifying health system response gaps to a potential emerging pathogen, if there is a strong mis- or disinformation culture, then improvements or changes to the nation\220s healthcare system \(whether preparatory or responsive\) may be negatively received. Even worse, when an outbreak does occur, there may not be enough trust in government or in its science to convince the public that a health emergency is actually occurring or that a vaccine is both safe and necessary to prevent further disease spread. Many experts highlight that the prevalence of social media and AI in modern society and communications has heightened the threat of misinformation and has contributed to an overall lack of trust in government, especially on health-related issues. )/Pg 54 0 R/K[3 4]>>
endobj

831 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/T(Another consideration external to a PRA tool is public mistrust in government surveillance, which is often necessary to provide the data required to properly recognize risk and measure preparedness. Moreover, in the modern era, there is widespread concern about personal protected information and data privacy, especially when government agencies are the end-users of such information. As an increasing number of people become uncomfortable with sharing their data\204whether due to fear of retribution, disillusionment in the government\220s ability to respond to crises, or confusion as to where and how that data is being used\204public trust declines, as does the availability of public health information. While these can be valid concerns, they do have an adverse effect on predictive tools that depend on such data to make accurate predictions about future risk and claims about present-day preparedness and health system capacity.)/E(Another consideration external to a PRA tool is public mistrust in government surveillance, which is often necessary to provide the data required to properly recognize risk and measure preparedness. Moreover, in the modern era, there is widespread concern about personal protected information and data privacy, especially when government agencies are the end-users of such information. As an increasing number of people become uncomfortable with sharing their data\204whether due to fear of retribution, disillusionment in the government\220s ability to respond to crises, or confusion as to where and how that data is being used\204public trust declines, as does the availability of public health information. While these can be valid concerns, they do have an adverse effect on predictive tools that depend on such data to make accurate predictions about future risk and claims about present-day preparedness and health system capacity.)/Pg 54 0 R/K[5]>>
endobj

832 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/T(Mis- and Disinformation Landscape)/E(Mis- and Disinformation Landscape)/Pg 54 0 R/K[7]>>
endobj

833 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/T(Public Mistrust in Surveillance)/E(Public Mistrust in Surveillance)/Pg 54 0 R/K[8]>>
endobj

834 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/Figure/P 320 0 R/Pg 54 0 R/K[10]>>
endobj

835 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/T(Policymakers are People Too)/E(Policymakers are People Too)/Pg 55 0 R/K[4]>>
endobj

836 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/T(Finally, policymakers have their own priorities and beliefs which may bias which issues they choose to support, and how they choose to do so. The COVID-19 pandemic is just one example of this, where the pandemic responses of federal governments and subnational and local leaders varied widely in public messaging, investments in vaccines, testing, treatment infrastructure, lockdown protocols, and other critical area. These individual priorities and beliefs do not just alter a policymaker\220s response to an existing pandemic but also their interest in preparing for and combatting a future epidemic or pandemic. Moreover, policymakers may be more or less likely to use a pandemic preparedness or risk tool depending on their personal agenda and beliefs about science and public health. These are just three examples of factors external to a PRA or pandemic preparedness-related tool that may impact the tool\220s utility and reception among end-users and the general public. )/E(Finally, policymakers have their own priorities and beliefs which may bias which issues they choose to support, and how they choose to do so. The COVID-19 pandemic is just one example of this, where the pandemic responses of federal governments and subnational and local leaders varied widely in public messaging, investments in vaccines, testing, treatment infrastructure, lockdown protocols, and other critical area. These individual priorities and beliefs do not just alter a policymaker\220s response to an existing pandemic but also their interest in preparing for and combatting a future epidemic or pandemic. Moreover, policymakers may be more or less likely to use a pandemic preparedness or risk tool depending on their personal agenda and beliefs about science and public health. These are just three examples of factors external to a PRA or pandemic preparedness-related tool that may impact the tool\220s utility and reception among end-users and the general public. )/Pg 55 0 R/K[0 1]>>
endobj

837 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/T(\215We like to think of misinformation as keyboard warriors in their basements, but it\220s actually this well-oiled machine.\216 - Expert Consultant)/E(\215We like to think of misinformation as keyboard warriors in their basements, but it\220s actually this well-oiled machine.\216 - Expert Consultant)/Pg 55 0 R/K[3]>>
endobj

