854 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/T(Mixed Ownership)/E(Mixed Ownership)/Pg 57 0 R/K[6]>>
endobj

855 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H4/T(At the foundational level, we recommend that a next-generation PRA tool integrate mixed governance among global, regional, and national actors. This would most effectively balance legitimacy, comparability, and political buy-in, especially at a time when multilateral consortiums like the WHO public trust concerns. )/E(At the foundational level, we recommend that a next-generation PRA tool integrate mixed governance among global, regional, and national actors. This would most effectively balance legitimacy, comparability, and political buy-in, especially at a time when multilateral consortiums like the WHO public trust concerns. )/Pg 57 0 R/K[4 5]>>
endobj

856 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/T(2.)/E(2.)/Pg 57 0 R/K[10]>>
endobj

857 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/T(Clear Purpose)/E(Clear Purpose)/Pg 57 0 R/K[9]>>
endobj

858 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H4/T(Political actors should publicly share the PRA\220s mandate of tracking evolving risk conditions and distinguish its intended goals from other tools that benchmark preparedness. This recommendation is aimed at circumventing public misinterpretation or misjudgement of PRA intent and outputs.)/E(Political actors should publicly share the PRA\220s mandate of tracking evolving risk conditions and distinguish its intended goals from other tools that benchmark preparedness. This recommendation is aimed at circumventing public misinterpretation or misjudgement of PRA intent and outputs.)/Pg 57 0 R/K[8]>>
endobj

859 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/T(Data Governance)/E(Data Governance)/Pg 57 0 R/K[13]>>
endobj

860 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/T(3.)/E(3.)/Pg 57 0 R/K[14]>>
endobj

861 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H4/T(The framework should explicitly quantify uncertainty by presenting confidence intervals visibly with ranges, scores, and clearly stated assumptions. Thus, decision-makers can calibrate relevant actions to evidentiary strength. )/E(The framework should explicitly quantify uncertainty by presenting confidence intervals visibly with ranges, scores, and clearly stated assumptions. Thus, decision-makers can calibrate relevant actions to evidentiary strength. )/Pg 57 0 R/K[11 12]>>
endobj

862 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/T(4.)/E(4.)/Pg 57 0 R/K[17]>>
endobj

863 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/T(Transparency)/E(Transparency)/Pg 57 0 R/K[16]>>
endobj

864 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H4/T(All methods, data sources, weighting choices, and key assumptions should be transparently documented. If proprietary inputs cannot be disclosed, their specific use cases should be disclosed.)/E(All methods, data sources, weighting choices, and key assumptions should be transparently documented. If proprietary inputs cannot be disclosed, their specific use cases should be disclosed.)/Pg 57 0 R/K[15]>>
endobj

865 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/Figure/P 323 0 R/Pg 57 0 R/K[19]>>
endobj

866 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/T(Architectural and Operational Recommendations)/E(Architectural and Operational Recommendations)/Pg 58 0 R/K[0]>>
endobj

867 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/T(5.)/E(5.)/Pg 58 0 R/K[4]>>
endobj

868 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/T(Subnational Integration)/E(Subnational Integration)/Pg 58 0 R/K[3]>>
endobj

869 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/T(Locally relevant, regional and subnational data, even if incomplete, needs to be integrated to avoid the misleading picture often produced by national aggregates. To do so, the framework must have a clear strategy on how it will incorporate and interpret subnational signals, especially where data is limited, delayed, or of substandard quality. Without such granularity, risk characterizations will continue to be mismatched with realities as they exist on the front lines.)/E(Locally relevant, regional and subnational data, even if incomplete, needs to be integrated to avoid the misleading picture often produced by national aggregates. To do so, the framework must have a clear strategy on how it will incorporate and interpret subnational signals, especially where data is limited, delayed, or of substandard quality. Without such granularity, risk characterizations will continue to be mismatched with realities as they exist on the front lines.)/Pg 58 0 R/K[2]>>
endobj

870 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/T(1.)/E(1.)/Pg 58 0 R/K[1]>>
endobj

871 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/T(6.)/E(6.)/Pg 58 0 R/K[7]>>
endobj

872 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/T(Sociopolitical Signals)/E(Sociopolitical Signals)/Pg 58 0 R/K[6]>>
endobj

873 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/T(The PRA should also incorporate sociopolitical signals like trust and polarization as core outbreak trajectory determinants. Trust, coherence of governance, susceptibility to misinformation, political polarization, and institutional stability are not peripheral factors but integral stages within the risk cascade. These elements influence detection, reporting, adherence to public health measures, capacity to mobilize resources, and the robustness of essential services. Although they are more challenging to quantify than traditional preparedness indicators, omitting them would yield a misleading characterization of risk. A next-generation PRA should, therefore, treat these areas as core components of analysis in the model rather than as extraneous modifiers.)/E(The PRA should also incorporate sociopolitical signals like trust and polarization as core outbreak trajectory determinants. Trust, coherence of governance, susceptibility to misinformation, political polarization, and institutional stability are not peripheral factors but integral stages within the risk cascade. These elements influence detection, reporting, adherence to public health measures, capacity to mobilize resources, and the robustness of essential services. Although they are more challenging to quantify than traditional preparedness indicators, omitting them would yield a misleading characterization of risk. A next-generation PRA should, therefore, treat these areas as core components of analysis in the model rather than as extraneous modifiers.)/Pg 58 0 R/K[5]>>
endobj

874 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/T(7.)/E(7.)/Pg 58 0 R/K[10]>>
endobj

875 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/T(Environmental Risk)/E(Environmental Risk)/Pg 58 0 R/K[9]>>
endobj

876 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/T(Environmental and ecological drivers should be also incorporated as structural components of the risk landscape. Climate-related pressures, habitat fragmentation, land-use change, and antimicrobial resistance are significantly influencing the conditions of pathogen emergence and transmission. To render these dynamics as optional variables overlooks the scientific consensus that such drivers directly impact spillover frequency, vector behavior, and pathogen evolution, in addition to burdening health systems. A more realistic PRA would place such factors at the center of its analytic framework and consider them to be fundamental drivers of future hazard probability.)/E(Environmental and ecological drivers should be also incorporated as structural components of the risk landscape. Climate-related pressures, habitat fragmentation, land-use change, and antimicrobial resistance are significantly influencing the conditions of pathogen emergence and transmission. To render these dynamics as optional variables overlooks the scientific consensus that such drivers directly impact spillover frequency, vector behavior, and pathogen evolution, in addition to burdening health systems. A more realistic PRA would place such factors at the center of its analytic framework and consider them to be fundamental drivers of future hazard probability.)/Pg 58 0 R/K[8]>>
endobj

877 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/T(8.)/E(8.)/Pg 58 0 R/K[12]>>
endobj

878 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/T(Dynamic Updating)/E(Dynamic Updating)/Pg 58 0 R/K[13]>>
endobj

879 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/T(To track these variables, the PRA should dynamically update in real time with automated feeds and versioned updates. When tools like these are static, it is difficult for end-users to interpret outputs as timely enough to warrant action. Since pandemics rely on such dynamic variables, including political landscapes and climate change, it is important that all outputs reflect that level of updating.)/E(To track these variables, the PRA should dynamically update in real time with automated feeds and versioned updates. When tools like these are static, it is difficult for end-users to interpret outputs as timely enough to warrant action. Since pandemics rely on such dynamic variables, including political landscapes and climate change, it is important that all outputs reflect that level of updating.)/Pg 58 0 R/K[11]>>
endobj

880 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/Figure/P 324 0 R/Pg 58 0 R/K[14]>>
endobj

881 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/T(9.)/E(9.)/Pg 59 0 R/K[3]>>
endobj

882 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/T(Modularity)/E(Modularity)/Pg 59 0 R/K[2]>>
endobj

883 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/T(To best implement these measures, the system should modularly link separate components \(e.g., spillover/ecology, health-system stress, sociopolitical vulnerability, antimicrobial resistance, climate disruption, and biosafety/biosecurity\). The limitations identified in the current generation of tools suggest that no one architecture can simultaneously capture spillover risk, laboratory incidents, socio-political vulnerability, antimicrobial resistance, climate-related disruption, and health system stress. By allowing flexibility in inputs, the PRA can facilitate more context-specific analyses that can be of greater use to policymakers and other key stakeholders. )/E(To best implement these measures, the system should modularly link separate components \(e.g., spillover/ecology, health-system stress, sociopolitical vulnerability, antimicrobial resistance, climate disruption, and biosafety/biosecurity\). The limitations identified in the current generation of tools suggest that no one architecture can simultaneously capture spillover risk, laboratory incidents, socio-political vulnerability, antimicrobial resistance, climate-related disruption, and health system stress. By allowing flexibility in inputs, the PRA can facilitate more context-specific analyses that can be of greater use to policymakers and other key stakeholders. )/Pg 59 0 R/K[0 1]>>
endobj

884 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/T(Outputs and Decision-Use )/E(Outputs and Decision-Use )/Pg 59 0 R/K[4]>>
endobj

885 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/T(10.)/E(10.)/Pg 59 0 R/K[8]>>
endobj

886 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/T(Tailored Outputs)/E(Tailored Outputs)/Pg 59 0 R/K[7]>>
endobj

887 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/T(The PRA should produce tailored outputs for different end users. These include finance ministries, public health agencies, multilateral organizations, funders, and research groups. An optimal PRA model will deliver consistent underlying information to each end user, but vary the framing, granularity, and decision hooks for each workflow. )/E(The PRA should produce tailored outputs for different end users. These include finance ministries, public health agencies, multilateral organizations, funders, and research groups. An optimal PRA model will deliver consistent underlying information to each end user, but vary the framing, granularity, and decision hooks for each workflow. )/Pg 59 0 R/K[5 6]>>
endobj

888 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/T(11.)/E(11.)/Pg 59 0 R/K[11]>>
endobj

889 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/T(Economic Risk Layer)/E(Economic Risk Layer)/Pg 59 0 R/K[10]>>
endobj

890 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/T(For funding prioritization, the modular PRA should translate risk into cost-of-inaction, fiscal exposure, investment logic, and ROI where feasible. As emphasized earlier, the need for quantifiable impact matters to stakeholders making decisions on how to allocate budgets\204and galvanising them to invest in future preparedness requires a snapshot of the economic benefits of strengthening preparedness that is backed by robust data.)/E(For funding prioritization, the modular PRA should translate risk into cost-of-inaction, fiscal exposure, investment logic, and ROI where feasible. As emphasized earlier, the need for quantifiable impact matters to stakeholders making decisions on how to allocate budgets\204and galvanising them to invest in future preparedness requires a snapshot of the economic benefits of strengthening preparedness that is backed by robust data.)/Pg 59 0 R/K[9]>>
endobj

891 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/T(12.)/E(12.)/Pg 59 0 R/K[14]>>
endobj

892 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/T(Tiered Triggers)/E(Tiered Triggers)/Pg 59 0 R/K[13]>>
endobj

893 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/T(The framework should operationalize results. Tiered triggers should link each risk tier to pre-specified policy steps like surveillance escalation, targeted preparedness investments, surge financing, or predefined response playbooks. Thus, the PRA can produce directly actionable outputs. These policy steps can subsequently be adjusted for specific local contexts, such as financial capacity, while providing a starting point for policymakers to mitigate risk.)/E(The framework should operationalize results. Tiered triggers should link each risk tier to pre-specified policy steps like surveillance escalation, targeted preparedness investments, surge financing, or predefined response playbooks. Thus, the PRA can produce directly actionable outputs. These policy steps can subsequently be adjusted for specific local contexts, such as financial capacity, while providing a starting point for policymakers to mitigate risk.)/Pg 59 0 R/K[12]>>
endobj

894 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/Figure/P 325 0 R/Pg 59 0 R/K[15]>>
endobj

895 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/T(13.)/E(13.)/Pg 60 0 R/K[3]>>
endobj

896 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/T(Institutionalizing)/E(Institutionalizing)/Pg 60 0 R/K[2]>>
endobj

897 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/T(These design choices only matter if we embed the PRA in an independent decision-relevant institution. Thus, the institution housing the PRA must maintain scientific rigor, stable financing, and durable links to pandemic surveillance and disaster risk reduction authorities. Without those institutional norms, risk assessment will continue to surge during crises and erode afterward. Such cyclical norms limit the data, expertise retention, and operational memory necessary for sustaining pandemic preparedness and prevention.)/E(These design choices only matter if we embed the PRA in an independent decision-relevant institution. Thus, the institution housing the PRA must maintain scientific rigor, stable financing, and durable links to pandemic surveillance and disaster risk reduction authorities. Without those institutional norms, risk assessment will continue to surge during crises and erode afterward. Such cyclical norms limit the data, expertise retention, and operational memory necessary for sustaining pandemic preparedness and prevention.)/Pg 60 0 R/K[0]>>
endobj

898 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/T(\215Global indices only [provide] benefit if they link to very specific functions that matter during an outbreak. When push comes to shove, there\220s nothing there that makes a big difference in terms of what you\220re [going to] do next.\216 - Expert Consultant)/E(\215Global indices only [provide] benefit if they link to very specific functions that matter during an outbreak. When push comes to shove, there\220s nothing there that makes a big difference in terms of what you\220re [going to] do next.\216 - Expert Consultant)/Pg 60 0 R/K[1]>>
endobj

