909 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/E(Our examination, as outlined above, has highlighted an urgent need for a next generation PRA framework that effectively integrates environmental, governmental, sociopolitical, and scientific data and communicates pandemic risk to a diverse range of key stakeholders. )/Pg 62 0 R/K[7 8]>>
endobj

910 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/T(Existing preparedness and risk tools fall short in several critical areas, which ultimately stifles the global community\220s ability to mount a holistic preparedness effort against emerging pandemic threats. Specifically, these tools suffer from an overreliance on static variables; a lack of subnational granularity; a failure to acknowledge the interplay between political, biological, technological, and environmental factors; and an ineffective means of communicating risk that prevents the proper facilitation of necessary policy changes. Economic factors such as resilience against shocks and sectoral interdependence are also critical to consider as risk factors but are not covered by existing tools, as outlined above. )/E(Existing preparedness and risk tools fall short in several critical areas, which ultimately stifles the global community\220s ability to mount a holistic preparedness effort against emerging pandemic threats. Specifically, these tools suffer from an overreliance on static variables; a lack of subnational granularity; a failure to acknowledge the interplay between political, biological, technological, and environmental factors; and an ineffective means of communicating risk that prevents the proper facilitation of necessary policy changes. Economic factors such as resilience against shocks and sectoral interdependence are also critical to consider as risk factors but are not covered by existing tools, as outlined above. )/Pg 62 0 R/K[0 1]>>
endobj

911 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/T(As such, a next generation PRA tool must integrate high-quality data pertaining to biological, sociopolitical, environmental, and governmental factors. Additionally, the tool will necessitate regular updates to account for emerging threats, such as AI and anthropogenic environmental changes. Lastly, the tool would benefit from a modular structure which allows for customization of the model for specific regions and contexts. This design feature, in addition to the others outlined above, will allow a new PRA tool to be maximally flexible to a variety of contributing risk factors, producing more accurate and reliable estimates of pandemic risk.)/E(As such, a next generation PRA tool must integrate high-quality data pertaining to biological, sociopolitical, environmental, and governmental factors. Additionally, the tool will necessitate regular updates to account for emerging threats, such as AI and anthropogenic environmental changes. Lastly, the tool would benefit from a modular structure which allows for customization of the model for specific regions and contexts. This design feature, in addition to the others outlined above, will allow a new PRA tool to be maximally flexible to a variety of contributing risk factors, producing more accurate and reliable estimates of pandemic risk.)/Pg 62 0 R/K[2]>>
endobj

912 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/T(It is important to note that the rollout of a next-generation PRA tool will probably face a number of challenges, including fractured policy environments polarized by mis- and disinformation, distrust in surveillance and data collection, privacy concerns, and competing political and funding priorities. As such, it is critical to ensure that the stakeholder\(s\) charged with overseeing the PRA tool and disseminating its outputs is able to cultivate public and private trust, garner sustained funding and support for continual and robust data collection, and has the institutional capacity to facilitate consistent and timely updating to the tool as new pandemic risk factors emerge. )/E(It is important to note that the rollout of a next-generation PRA tool will probably face a number of challenges, including fractured policy environments polarized by mis- and disinformation, distrust in surveillance and data collection, privacy concerns, and competing political and funding priorities. As such, it is critical to ensure that the stakeholder\(s\) charged with overseeing the PRA tool and disseminating its outputs is able to cultivate public and private trust, garner sustained funding and support for continual and robust data collection, and has the institutional capacity to facilitate consistent and timely updating to the tool as new pandemic risk factors emerge. )/Pg 62 0 R/K[3 4 5]>>
endobj

